---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CTA from "../components/CTA.astro";
import { countries } from "../data/places";
import { breadcrumbs } from "../lib/schema";
---
<Base
  title="Is It Legal? Country-by-Country Peptide Status"
  description="Research chemical status, regulator and customs position for 15 countries. No prescription, no import licence at personal quantity."
  path="/legality/"
  schema={[breadcrumbs([{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Legality", url: "/legality/" }])]}
>
  <p class="eyebrow">15 countries · Stated plainly</p>
  <h1>Legality by country</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Nothing here is sold as a licensed medicine. It is sold as a research chemical, which is why there is no
    prescription and no pharmacy registration. Below is the actual position per country, regulator named.
  </p>

  <h2>Country table</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Regulator</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {countries.map((c) => (
        <tr><td>{c.name}</td><td class="mono">{c.regulator}</td><td>{c.legal}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What "research chemical" actually means</h2>
  <p>
    It means the vial is not a finished medicinal product with a marketing authorisation. It has not been through
    a licensing file. The label says laboratory use, and dosing is your call rather than a doctor's. That is the
    trade for the price: the same molecule without the licensing overhead.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does not mean</h2>
  <p>
    It does not mean unregulated or anything-goes. Every country above has a named regulator, and those
    regulators have positions. In the EU the position at personal quantity is consistently permissive. Sweden
    and Ireland are the strictest on paper and still pass personal vials. Switzerland and the UK are outside the
    EU and require a customs declaration, which we make.
  </p>

  <h2>Prescription question</h2>
  <p>
    Semaglutide and tirzepatide are prescription medicines when dispensed as Ozempic, Mounjaro or Wegovy. The
    vials here are not those products. They are the same molecule supplied as a research chemical, which is why
    they cost a fraction and arrive without a pharmacy label.
  </p>

  <h2>The compliance line</h2>
  <p>
    Research chemicals, not a pharmacy, not medical advice. If a doctor is managing you, tell them what you are
    taking.
  </p>

  <CTA
    src="/legality/"
    label="Legality page"
    heading="Ask about your country"
    body="One message, straight answer on the customs and regulator position where you live."
  />
</Base>